



  <section xml:id="Math2301Day16">
    <title>Quadratic Algebra</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-day16-review">
      <title>Review</title>

      <p>
        What was the statement of the spectral theorem?
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-symmetry-without-coordinates">
      <title>Symmetry without coordinates</title>

      <p>
        It turns out that eigenvectors and eigenvalues are particularly nice for <term>symmetric matrices</term>, matrices such that <m>A = A^{\intercal}</m>.
        We want to describe this in arbitrary vector spaces, and this is particularly important as most of what we do here extends to infinite dimensions.
        In quantum mechanics, you study infinite dimensional operators, and a key property is the following:
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-self-adjoint">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A linear operator <m>T</m> on an inner-product space <m>V</m> is <term>self-adjoint</term> if
            <me>
              \langle T(\vec{u}), \vec{v}\rangle = \langle \vec{u},T(\vec{v})\rangle
              \qquad\text{for all }\vec{u},\vec{v}\in V.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        In an orthonormal basis on a finite dimensional inner product space, <m>T</m> is self-adjoint exactly when its matrix <m>A</m> is symmetric:
        <me>
          A^{\intercal}=A.
        </me>
      </p>

      <!--
      Indeed, in standard coordinates,
      \ip{A\vec{x}}{\vec{y}}=\vec{y}^{\intercal} A\vec{x}=\vec{x}^{\intercal} A^{\intercal} \vec{y},
      \qquad
      \ip{\vec{x}}{A\vec{y}}=\vec{x}^{\intercal} A\vec{y}.
      Equality for all \vec{x},\vec{y} is equivalent to A^{\intercal}=A.
      -->

      <p>
        This definition turns out to be advantageous for generalization and for proving things.
        For example, let's recall projection operations.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-projection-self-adjoint">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>P_{W}: V \to V</m> be an orthogonal projection onto a linear subspace <m>W</m> of an inner product space <m>V</m>.
            Then <m>P_{W}</m> is self-adjoint.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p>
            Let <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in V</m>.
            Using orthogonal decomposition, <m>\vec{u} = \vec{u}_{1} + \vec{u}_{2}</m> and <m>\vec{v} = \vec{v}_{1} + \vec{v}_{2}</m>, where <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \vec{v}_{1} \in W</m> and <m>\vec{u}_{2}, \vec{v}_{2} \in W^{\perp}</m>.
            Then applying orthogonality relations and these decompositions yields:
            <md>
              <mrow>\ip{P_{W}(\vec{u})}{\vec{v}} \amp = \ip{\vec{u}_{1}}{\vec{v}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \ip{\vec{u}_{1}}{\vec{v}_{1} + \vec{v}_{2}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \ip{\vec{u}_{1}}{\vec{v}_{1}} + \ip{\vec{u}_{1}}{\vec{v}_{2}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \ip{\vec{u}_{1}}{\vec{v}_{1}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \ip{\vec{u}_{1}}{\vec{v}_{1}} + \ip{\vec{u}_{2}}{\vec{v}_{1}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \ip{\vec{u}_{1} + \vec{u}_{2}}{\vec{v}_{1}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp = \ip{\vec{u}}{P_{W}(\vec{v})}.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-eigenspaces-perpendicular">
      <title>Different eigenspaces are perpendicular</title>

      <p>
        Being self-adjoint allows us to not only show that eigenspaces are distinct and independent but, in fact, orthogonal:
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-eigenvectors-orthogonal">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T</m> be a self-adjoint operator on an inner product space <m>V</m>.
            Let <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in V</m> be eigenvectors with distinct eigenvalues in <m>V</m>.
            Then <m>\vec{u}\perp \vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p>
            Let <m>T(\vec{u}) = \lambda\vec{u}</m> and <m>T(\vec{v}) = \mu\vec{v}</m>.
            Self-adjointness gives
            <me>
              \lambda\ip{\vec{u}}{\vec{v}}=\ip{\lambda \vec{u}}{\vec{v}}=\ip{T(\vec{u})}{\vec{v}}
              =\ip{\vec{u}}{T(\vec{v})}=\ip{\vec{u}}{\mu \vec{v}}=\mu\ip{\vec{u}}{\vec{v}}.
            </me>
            Thus <m>(\lambda-\mu)\ip{\vec{u}}{\vec{v}}=0</m>.
            Since the eigenvalues are distinct <m>\lambda - \mu \neq 0</m>, so <m>\ip{\vec{u}}{\vec{v}}=0</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <!--
      <example>
        <title>Symmetry produces perpendicular eigendirections</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}3\amp 1\\1\amp 3\end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>(1,1)</m> and <m>(1,-1)</m> have eigenvalues <m>4</m> and <m>2</m>, and their dot product is <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      -->
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-quadratic-forms">
      <title>Quadratic Forms</title>

      <p>
        Up until this point in the eigen-unit, we have proven some new things about stuff we already know, but the main things we are talking about are pretty familiar from Math 2000.
        In order to prove the deeper results, we will need to go one step beyond linear algebra to <alert>quadratic algebra</alert>.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-quadratic-form">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <term>quadratic form</term> associated with a matrix <m>A</m> is
            <me>
              q_A(\vec{x})=\ip{\vec{x}}{A\vec{x}}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        More explicitly for an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, we get
        <me>
          q_{A}(\vec{x}) = \sum_{1 \leq i, j \leq n} A_{i,j} x_{i} x_{j}.
        </me>
        Notation you will also see for this is <m>q_{A}(\vec{x}) = \vec{x}^{\intercal} A \vec{x}</m>.
        If you treat <m>\vec{x}</m> as an <m>n \times 1</m> vector, this gives precisely the same formula as the standard inner product on <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each term has degree two, which is where it gets its name.
        Quadratic forms are a key tool for studying symmetric matrices.
        This is because they only care about the symmetric part of a matrix.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-quadratic-form-symmetric-part">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix.
            Then for all <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m>,
            <me>
              q_{A}(\vec{x}) = q_{S}(\vec{x}),
            </me>
            where <m>S = (A + A^{\intercal})/2</m>.
            In particular, <m>S</m> is symmetric.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p>
            Note that
            <me>
              A=S+K, \text{ where }
              \qquad
              S=\frac{A+A^{\intercal}}{2},
              \qquad
              K=\frac{A-A^{\intercal}}{2}.
            </me>
            Then, since transposing is a linear involution (i.e., it is its own inverse) <m>S</m> is symmetric and <m>K = -K^{\intercal}</m>, which we call <term>skew symmetric</term>.
            Let <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
            Note that, by bilinearity,
            <me>
              q_{A}(\vec{x}) = \ip{\vec{x}}{A \vec{x}} = \ip{\vec{x}}{S \vec{x}} + \ip{\vec{x}}{K \vec{x}}.
            </me>
            Thus, to complete the proof, it suffices to show that <m>\ip{\vec{x}}{K\vec{x}} = 0</m>.
            Observe that
            <me>
              \ip{\vec{x}}{K\vec{x}} = \ip{K^{\intercal} \vec{x}}{\vec{x}} = \ip{-K \vec{x}}{\vec{x}} = \ip{\vec{x}}{-K\vec{x}} = -\ip{\vec{x}}{K\vec{x}}.
            </me>
            Thus, <m>\ip{\vec{x}}{K\vec{x}} = 0</m>, which completes the proof.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <!--
      Since the scalar \vec{x}^{\intercal} K\vec{x} equals its transpose,
      \vec{x}^{\intercal} K\vec{x}=(\vec{x}^{\intercal} K\vec{x})^{\intercal}=\vec{x}^{\intercal} K^{\intercal} \vec{x}=-\vec{x}^{\intercal} K\vec{x},
      we get \vec{x}^{\intercal} K\vec{x}=0.  Therefore
      q_A(\vec{x})=q_S(\vec{x}).
      -->

      <p>
        Quadratic measurements cannot detect the skew-symmetric part of a matrix.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-positive-matrices">
      <title>Positive Matrices</title>

      <p>
        A quadratic form gives a way to detect when an operator is positive.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-psd-pd">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>positive semidefinite</term> (PSD) if <m>q_{A}(\vec{x}) \ge 0</m> for every <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m>;
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>positive definite</term> (PD) if <m>q_{A}(\vec{x}) \gt 0</m> for every nonzero <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        For example, the identity matrix is positive definite, since
        <me>
          q_{I_{n}}(\vec{x}) = \ip{\vec{x}}{\vec{x}} \geq 0
        </me>
        with equality if and only if <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.
      </p>


    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-day16">
      <title>Exercises</title>

      <exercise>
      <p> Let <m>A</m> be a positive semidefinite matrix. Show that all eigenvalues of A are non-negative. </p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-outer-product">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that the <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>\vec{x}\vec{x}^{\intercal}</m> satisfies
              <me>
                (\vec{x}\vec{x}^{\intercal})_{i,j} = x_{i}x_{j}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that <m>\vec{x}\vec{x}^{\intercal}</m> is symmetric.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that <m>\vec{x}\vec{x}^{\intercal}</m> is positive semidefinite.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-pd-cone">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be a positive definite matrix.
            Show that
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For any <m>c \gt 0</m>, <m>cA</m> is positive definite.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              For any positive definite matrix <m>B</m>, <m>A + B</m> is positive definite.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-combination-outer-products">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that any non-negative linear combination of matrices of the form <m>\vec{x}\vec{x}^{\intercal}</m> is positive semidefinite.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <conclusion>
        <p>
          It turns out the spectral theorem shows that the reverse direction is true: every positive semidefinite matrix is a non-negative combination of matrices of the form <m>\vec{x}\vec{x}^{\intercal}</m>.
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </exercises>

  </section>

